## Problem

The System Brief can define the runner's module boundaries, but the coding agent must not infer each module's algorithms, signatures, state transitions, errors, or edge-case behavior during GREEN.

Every module selected by the accepted NIB-S therefore requires a decisional-complete Module Brief before production implementation.

## Current authoritative boundary

Module Briefs consume the accepted NIB-S and existing TURNLOCK hostile-review authority.

They may implement accepted behavior but may not introduce product semantics, alter the campaign protocol, or invent behavior to accommodate a provider or implementation convenience.

## Scope

Produce the complete set of active NIB-M documents required by the accepted NIB-S.

For every module selected by NIB-S, the architect must specify:

- exact responsibility;
- exact public/internal signatures required by the construction design;
- exact input/output types;
- state read/write ownership;
- implementable algorithm/pseudocode;
- deterministic versus cognitive operations;
- error taxonomy and propagation;
- retry behavior where applicable;
- idempotence behavior;
- edge cases;
- invariants;
- interactions with sealed artifacts and campaign state;
- required dependency-contract references;
- behavior under interruption/restart where relevant;
- forbidden behavior.

If one accepted module has multiple independently coherent responsibilities that require separate briefs, the architect determines and records that decomposition. The coding agent does not.

## Exclusions

This Issue does not:

- write production code;
- write the NIB-T;
- implement tests;
- choose new module boundaries contrary to the accepted NIB-S;
- create dependency behavior not already selected by NIB-S;
- select real reviewer profiles;
- execute a real campaign.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Every implementation module selected by NIB-S has sufficient active NIB-M coverage.
- [ ] Module signatures and cross-module types are coherent with NIB-S.
- [ ] Every branch that production code must implement is determined by explicit module behavior or an authoritative dependency contract.
- [ ] Failure, retry, interruption, restart, and edge-case behavior are explicit where applicable.
- [ ] State ownership and mutation boundaries are explicit.
- [ ] No module brief requires the coding agent to choose campaign semantics.
- [ ] External non-trivial dependency usage is explicitly identified for Dependency Contract coverage.
- [ ] Inter-NIB coherence is checked across the complete module set.
- [ ] Every material discovery is classified and routed before acceptance.
- [ ] No production implementation is added.

## Validation

Perform an architect review for decisional completeness, non-ambiguity, exact compatibility with the accepted NIB-S, and complete coverage of the selected module architecture.

