- [ ] Every raw finding has exactly one normalized finding destination.
- [ ] No semantic merge or semantic deduplication occurs.
- [ ] Finding identity and provenance are exact and replay-stable.
- [ ] Materiality is derived only from the accepted seven-axis structure.
- [ ] Non-material closure requires the accepted hostile challenge path.
- [ ] Material refutation requires the accepted hostile challenge path.
- [ ] Challenge objections cannot be converted into approval by M5 judgment.
- [ ] Stale-protocol findings remain durable and are explicitly re-adjudicated under current protocol when required.
- [ ] Historical evidence is never rewritten.
- [ ] Adjudication and re-adjudication products cross M5 explicitly rather than being hidden behind WorkItems.
- [ ] Failure and integrity cases are explicit.
- [ ] No coding-agent semantic choice remains in M5-B scope.
- [ ] No production implementation is added.

## Authoritative references

- then-current active `NIB-S-GATE-A-CAMPAIGN-RUNNER`
- ADR-042 through ADR-048
- current review-evidence meta-schema
- current raw-review schema
- current challenge packet/output schemas
- current Gate A protocol bundle
- Issue #30

## Problem

A surviving material finding cannot be resolved by generic model judgment.

M5 must implement the accepted resolution branches that distinguish valid
refutation, uniquely derived correction, genuine semantic
underdetermination/authority conflict, and unresolved operational or epistemic
conditions.

The coding agent must not infer when a finding authorizes a repair, when a
Decision Request is legitimate, or when automatic progression must stop.

## Current authoritative boundary

Accepted hostile-review authority requires:

```text
failure to refute
!=
proof that finding is true

failure to derive correction
!=
DECISION-REQUIRED

DECISION-REQUIRED
==
genuine product-semantic underdetermination
or
genuine unresolved product-authority conflict
```

Automated repair is permitted only when the correction is uniquely derived from
existing authority, the derivation survives hostile challenge, the exact patch
is produced, and the exact patch survives repair challenge.

M5 may qualify semantic products but cannot create new product authority.

## Scope

Produce active Module Brief coverage for M5-C
`resolution qualification`.

Define exactly:

- surviving-material-finding intake;
- discovery-classifier work and artifact relationships;
- derivation-builder work;
- exact unique-derivation candidate representation;
- hostile challenge of unique derivation where required;
- decision-necessity-challenger work;
- exact classification of genuine product underdetermination;
- exact classification of unresolved product-authority conflict;
- rejection of fabricated `DECISION-REQUIRED`;
- repair-synthesizer work;
- exact repair proposal representation;
- exact repair challenge input/output binding;
- maximum repair/closure revision behavior inherited from current protocol;
- exact approved patch artifact construction;
- `RepairIntentRef` identity;
- `RepairIntentRef` qualification evidence;
- `RepairIntentRef` qualification artifacts;
- candidate binding of RepairIntent;
- prohibition on patch scope expansion;
- `DecisionRequestRef` identity;
- exact Decision Request artifact schema;
- binding of Decision Request to current full S, current full P, and exact finding;
- exact semantic `CampaignBlocker` / `SemanticBlocker` construction associated with a qualified Decision Request;
- unresolved operational/epistemic branch handoff through M5 producer-domain operational causes;
- obligation satisfaction/supersession generated by each terminal resolution branch;
- replay/idempotence;
- restart behavior;
- failure taxonomy;
- required invariants;
- forbidden behavior.

## Exclusions

This Issue does not:

- answer a Decision Request;
- accept new product semantics;
- apply repository patches;
- perform LLM calls directly;
- invoke Python validators;
- construct M8-B-owned OperationalBlocker identities;
- declare Gate A ready;
- implement production code.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Every surviving material finding enters one explicit accepted resolution path or remains explicitly unresolved.
- [ ] Failure to refute is never converted into proof of finding truth.
- [ ] Failure to derive a correction is never converted into `DECISION-REQUIRED`.
- [ ] `DECISION-REQUIRED` can be produced only from the accepted genuine semantic boundary.
- [ ] Unique derivation is bound to exact current authority and exact finding.
- [ ] Automated repair requires exact derivation and exact hostile challenge closure.
- [ ] Repair qualification produces one exact candidate-bound `RepairIntentRef`.
- [ ] The approved patch cannot silently broaden its authorized semantic scope.
- [ ] Decision Requests are bound to exact current S, current P, and finding.
- [ ] Semantic blocker construction is explicit and cannot be mistaken for an operational blocker.
- [ ] Operational/epistemic impossibility is handed to the accepted M5-to-M8-B cause boundary.
- [ ] Replay cannot duplicate RepairIntent, DecisionRequest, or semantic blockers.
- [ ] Failure and integrity cases are explicit.
- [ ] No coding-agent semantic choice remains in M5-C scope.
- [ ] No production implementation is added.

## Authoritative references

- then-current active `NIB-S-GATE-A-CAMPAIGN-RUNNER`
- ADR-041 through ADR-049
- current Gate A hostile-review protocol bundle
- current challenge contracts
- active M2 Module Briefs
- active M8-B Module Brief
- Issue #30

## Construction context correction already fixed upstream

The active System Brief now separates immutable `ReviewContext` from
current-candidate `ResolutionContextV1`.

M5-C must consume this boundary as follows:

- semantic resolution subjects are bound to exact current `S`, current `P`,
  exact finding provenance, and exact current adjudication/re-adjudication
  basis;
- review evidence and `ReviewContext` are never retargeted to a later
  `CandidateRevision`;
- M5-C resolution-role cognitive work uses `ResolutionContextV1`;
- semantic derivation/qualification is distinct from candidate-bound repair
  realization;
- `RepairIntentRef` and its approved patch are bound to exactly one candidate
  and may never be rebound/rebased to a successor;
- after a same-`(S, P)` candidate advance, a still-current semantic resolution
  may require fresh repair synthesis and fresh repair challenge against the new
  current candidate;
- at most one candidate-bound automatic repair realization may be enabled for
  materialization at a time;
- Issue #53 must define the exact deterministic repair-realization ordering; it
  must not depend on scheduler timing, execution completion order, confidence,
  severity, patch convenience, or model preference.

This correction changes construction architecture only. It does not change
TURNLOCK product semantics or hostile-review protocol semantics.

## Problem

M5 must eventually produce both the exact repository-facing assurance material
required by the existing Python Gate A authority and an exact
`CandidateReviewReadinessRef` for mechanical qualification.

The coding agent must not infer which assurance artifacts belong in the
candidate repository, their exact repository placement, how they become part of
an immutable candidate, which campaigns contribute to qualification, or when
review work is sufficiently closed to call M6.

## Current authoritative boundary

The campaign runner does not define a replacement Gate A acceptance algorithm.

M5 may determine that the accepted campaign work and evidence basis is ready to
be submitted to M6.

M6 then invokes existing Python repository/Gate A authority.

M5 does not independently declare `GATE-A-READY`.

Campaign applicability remains based on exact `(S, P)`, not candidate or
repository provenance.

Every current campaign selected by repository authority participates in the
current campaign basis.

Stale-protocol campaigns contribute only through the accepted current-protocol
re-adjudication mechanisms.

## Scope

Produce active Module Brief coverage for M5-D
`assurance repository projection / readiness`.

Define exactly:

- the M5-owned assurance repository projection representation selected by the
  then-current accepted System Brief;
- exact repository-relative placement of runner-produced hostile-review
  artifacts;
- exact mapping from immutable `ArtifactRef` values to repository projection
  entries;
- exact campaign/review record composition;
- exact execution-receipt references;
- exact supporting-execution relationships;
- exact finding/adjudication/re-adjudication projection;
- immutable review-evidence serialization;
- deterministic ordering;
- deterministic projection identity;
- duplicate/collision handling;
- prohibition on M5 direct repository mutation;
- mechanical M7 consumption of the exact projection;
- assurance-only candidate materialization behavior;
- repair-plus-assurance materialization behavior;
- subject-preservation requirement for assurance-only materialization;
- replay/idempotence across sealed projection and candidate materialization;
- complete current campaign-set derivation from exact `GateAEvaluationContext`;
- exact contributing-campaign-set derivation;
- stale-protocol contribution rules;
- exact `CandidateReviewReadinessRef` identity;
- exact `basisArtifacts` composition and ordering;
- readiness preconditions;
- outstanding-obligation requirements;
- unresolved-execution requirements;
- outstanding-blocker requirements;
- exact handoff to M6 gate-a-qualification;
- behavior after failed mechanical qualification;
- replay/idempotence;
- restart behavior;
- failure taxonomy;
- required invariants;
- forbidden behavior.

The brief must not reimplement Python Gate A validity logic.

## Exclusions

This Issue does not:

- implement production code;
- perform repository mutation directly from M5;
- define M7 Git mechanics;
- invoke Python validators;
- create a new Gate A acceptance algorithm;
- declare `GATE-A-READY`;
- change campaign-currentness semantics;
- change hostile-review protocol semantics;
- create TURNLOCK product semantics.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Every M5-produced repository assurance artifact has an exact deterministic repository projection.
- [ ] M5 never mutates the candidate repository directly.
- [ ] M7 can consume the exact projection mechanically without interpreting assurance meaning.
- [ ] Assurance-only candidate materialization cannot silently alter the reviewed semantic subject.
- [ ] Repair-plus-assurance materialization preserves exact RepairIntent and assurance provenance simultaneously.
- [ ] Review-evidence bytes and repository-relative paths are deterministic.
- [ ] Projection collision/duplicate behavior is explicit and fail-closed.
- [ ] `currentReviewCampaignIds` equals the complete repository-selected current campaign set over exact `(S, P)`.
- [ ] `contributingReviewCampaignIds` includes every and only accepted campaign contribution required by current evidence/re-adjudication basis.
- [ ] CandidateReviewReadiness identity and basis artifacts are deterministic.
- [ ] Readiness cannot exist while relevant campaign work, obligations, blockers, or unresolved executions can still change the qualification basis.
- [ ] M6 receives the exact candidate and exact CandidateReviewReadiness basis.
- [ ] M5 does not independently reproduce Python Gate A acceptance logic.
- [ ] Failure and integrity cases are explicit.
- [ ] No coding-agent semantic choice remains in M5-D scope.
- [ ] No production implementation is added.

## Authoritative references

- then-current active `NIB-S-GATE-A-CAMPAIGN-RUNNER`
- current hostile-review protocol/evidence contracts
- active M2 Module Briefs
- active M7 Module Briefs
- future accepted M5-A/M5-B/M5-C Module Briefs
- `formal/verification.yaml`
- Issue #30

content.body:
## Required outcome

Record and synchronize the product-owner decision that TURNLOCK Core is a
runtime-composable execution substrate for supported concrete execution
realizations that are not fixed by TURNLOCK semantics.

When an alternative realization can preserve the applicable TURNLOCK semantics,
modifying or recompiling Core must not be the only supported substitution path.

The work records this decision in ADR-030, adds TL-INV-038, synchronizes the
normative specification and formal traceability, and preserves existing
responsibility boundaries.

## Accepted product decision

The product decision is already accepted. This Issue does not adjudicate
alternatives.

A supported external execution realization that is not itself fixed by TURNLOCK
semantics must be composable with an already-built Core at runtime when doing so
is technically realizable without changing TURNLOCK semantics.

The realization must become effective before its intended causal use.

Once TURNLOCK has accepted the realization as governing an applicable use, the
runtime must not silently bypass it or substitute another realization unless the
applicable TURNLOCK semantics authorize that alternative.

## Discovery classification

The pre-decision ambiguity was `decision-required`.

ADR-019 established that evaluation and optimization policy remain outside Core
but did not establish runtime/binary composability with an already-built Core.

The product owner has explicitly selected runtime composability.

The derived runtime-realization obligation is an independent universal
obligation and is admitted as TL-INV-038.

## Existing authority preserved

- ADR-007: main-agent cognitive lineage remains first-class.
- ADR-010: semantics remain harness-independent.
- ADR-019 / TL-INV-034: evaluation and optimization policy remain outside Core.
- ADR-024/025/026 / TL-INV-036: effective-condition provenance remains capturable.
- ADR-029: TURNLOCK does not own general filesystem/tool/sandbox/ACL policy.
- ADR-027/028 / TL-INV-037: governing workflow definition remains stable.

## Required repository changes

- create accepted ADR-030;
- add normative product-intent section 0.13F;
- add TL-INV-038 as Section 3.36;
- add the corresponding architectural implication;
- preserve extension-mechanism choices as open;
- add TL-INV-038 to formal/verification.yaml as formalization: not-applicable;
- regenerate affected ADR/formal projections;
- update the non-authoritative synopsis only to reflect the new baseline;
- run the complete repository validation contract.

## Non-goals

This work does not select or require:

- a plugin system;
- hooks or callbacks;
- dependency injection;
- IPC/RPC/sockets;
- dynamic libraries;
- sidecars;
- a resource registry;
- arbitrary before-step or after-step interception;
- a Run, RunSpec, RunProof, ControlProfile, ExperimentProfile, or ControlStatus;
- activation IDs or run IDs;
- replay;
- cross-run comparability;
- evaluation metrics;
- optimizer policy;
- a general sandbox/permission system;
- replacement of main-agent continuation with a fresh agent;
- external control of every execution determinant.

## Acceptance criteria

- ADR-030 is accepted and synchronized.
- TL-INV-038 exists with the accepted runtime-realization wording.
- No second invariant is created for causal-control validity, session-state
  awareness, control status, scope fidelity, or experiment validity.
- TL-INV-038 is `formalization: not-applicable`.
- No artificial TLA+ mechanism is introduced.
- No concrete runtime extension mechanism is made normative.
- ADR-019, ADR-024/025/026, ADR-029, and TL-INV-037 remain semantically intact.
- The repository's generated ADR/formal projections are current.
- All required repository checks pass.

## Problem

TURNLOCK now has an accepted hostile-review assurance architecture and a content-addressed Gate A campaign protocol, but no campaign runner exists to execute that protocol end to end.

Gate A therefore cannot yet obtain real assurance-decomposition campaign evidence without manual orchestration.

The runner must operationalize the accepted assurance mechanics without becoming a new source of TURNLOCK semantics.

## Evidence and provenance

At repository revision `4be146f3e2d55a84f9ea638491c4f85a1e93f6fa`, the hostile-review protocol substrate is closed through ADR-048, protocol v4 is current, review evidence is schema 5.0, no real hostile-review campaign exists, and Gate A remains blocked on real campaign evidence.

The stable construction sequence is documented in `docs/formal/gate-a-campaign-runner-construction-plan.md`.

## Current authoritative boundary

Product meaning remains owned by `docs/specification/turnlock-spec.md` and accepted TURNLOCK ADRs.

The formal-assurance graph remains owned by `formal/verification.yaml`.

Hostile-review execution and evidence mechanics remain governed by ADR-041 through ADR-048 and the content-addressed artifacts under `formal/reviews/`.

The implementation may realize those responsibilities but may not alter or silently complete them.

Workspace construction mechanisms such as NIBs, Dependency Contracts, hostile construction-document review, local credential resolution, and implementation-quality review govern the construction process only. They are not campaign runtime semantics or Gate A evidence.

## Scope

This Issue owns construction of the Gate A campaign runner from implementation conception through RED, GREEN, and construction transition.

It includes the implementation required to:

- execute exact campaign inputs under the current semantic subject and review protocol;
- create and validate sealed content-addressed campaign artifacts;
- persist and recover campaign execution;
- execute bounded LLM calls through the selected runtime dependency;
- enforce reviewer-profile and model-identity rules;
- execute initial hostile reviewers;
- normalize findings mechanically;
- execute the accepted adjudication and hostile-challenge paths;
- route findings conservatively;
- execute exact authorized repair and human-decision paths;
- isolate repository/worktree mutation;
- handle subject/protocol staleness and restart;
- expose the top-level `run-gate-a-review` command and its three accepted external outcomes;
- establish the post-GREEN transition where code and tests become implementation truth.

The exact GREEN implementation Issue decomposition is created only after the NIB-M set has been completed and hostile-reviewed.

## Exclusions

This Issue does not:

- change TURNLOCK product semantics;
- introduce or amend a `TL-INV-*`;
- author the candidate canonical TLA+ model;
- run TLC;
- admit real Gate A reviewer profiles;
- select frontier models before the implemented runtime boundary can be exercised;
- execute the first real Gate A campaign;
- fabricate campaign evidence for implementation testing;
- use workspace construction hostile review as Gate A hostile-review evidence;
- make `dotagents`, a coding harness, or a local workspace skill part of TURNLOCK protocol semantics.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A complete active construction pack removes coding-agent design discretion before production implementation.
- [ ] The construction pack includes a coherent NIB-S, all required NIB-M briefs, required Dependency Contracts, and NIB-T.
- [ ] The construction pack receives hostile construction-document review and every material discovery is resolved or routed before RED.
- [ ] RED is established before production implementation.
- [ ] The complete accepted behavioral suite becomes GREEN through implementation.
- [ ] The implemented runner realizes every capability family required by the construction plan.
- [ ] Crash, restart, retry, sealing, stale-subject, stale-protocol, challenge, repair, decision, and publication failure paths are covered by executable tests.
- [ ] `run-gate-a-review` exposes only `GATE-A-READY`, `DECISION-REQUIRED`, and `OPERATOR-ACTION-REQUIRED` as normal external campaign outcomes.
- [ ] The implementation cannot create TURNLOCK product semantics or convert technical uncertainty into a product decision.
- [ ] Secret values do not enter repository artifacts, durable evidence, logs intended as evidence, Issues, or comments.
- [ ] Repository integrity and implementation-quality validation pass.
- [ ] Construction NIBs and Dependency Contracts complete their transition lifecycle and no longer compete with code/tests as current implementation authority.
- [ ] No real Gate A campaign is recorded by this construction work.

## Validation

Validation must include the repository-mandated integrity suite, the complete runner acceptance/property test suite, restart/failure-path tests, and the implementation-quality closure selected by the accepted construction design.

Every implementation increment must preserve the accepted hostile-review evidence contracts.

## Authoritative references

- `docs/specification/turnlock-spec.md`
- `docs/adr/adr-041-establish-turnlock-formal-assurance-architecture.md`
- `docs/adr/adr-042-define-auditable-hostile-review-campaign-execution-and-adjudication.md`
- `docs/adr/adr-043-bind-hostile-review-evidence-to-exact-reviewed-inputs.md`
- `docs/adr/adr-044-require-one-unambiguous-gate-a-review-subject.md`
- `docs/adr/adr-045-bind-gate-a-campaigns-to-versioned-review-protocol-and-derived-evidence.md`
- `docs/adr/adr-046-bind-challenge-executions-to-exact-inputs-and-derive-retry-admissibility.md`
- `docs/adr/adr-047-make-unvalidated-cognitive-completions-terminal-and-validate-readiness-projections.md`
- `docs/adr/adr-048-content-address-hostile-review-meta-schemas.md`
- `formal/verification.yaml`
- `formal/reviews/README.md`
- `docs/formal/gate-a-campaign-runner-construction-plan.md`

## Problem

The accepted hostile-review assurance protocol defines what a valid Gate A campaign must mean and preserve, but it does not yet define the complete implementation architecture of the campaign runner.

Production implementation must not begin while the coding agent would still need to choose system boundaries, module topology, state ownership, persistence behavior, runtime placement, or orchestration.

## Evidence and provenance

The campaign-runner construction sequence is documented in `docs/formal/gate-a-campaign-runner-construction-plan.md`.

The governing hostile-review architecture is recorded by ADR-041 through ADR-048.

No production campaign runner currently exists.

## Current authoritative boundary

The NIB-S may select implementation realization choices only within the behavior already authorized by TURNLOCK authority and the hostile-review assurance contract.

It may not create product semantics, weaken or strengthen Gate A evidence rules, select convenience behavior where current authority is ambiguous, or treat a provider/harness capability as semantic authority.

Any durable contestable implementation-architecture decision whose rationale must outlive construction must be routed into the appropriate permanent repository authority before the NIB-S relies on it.

## Scope

Produce the complete active System Brief for the Gate A campaign runner.

The architect must determine before mechanical materialization:

- implementation ecosystem and language boundary;
- package/application placement;
- top-level executable/CLI boundary;
- complete pipeline architecture;
- exact module responsibilities;
- exact cross-module input/output types;
- campaign identity representation;
- state-machine boundary;
- persistence and campaign-journal boundary;
- sealed-artifact storage boundary;
- restart and idempotence semantics;
- concurrency and ownership rules;
- semantic-subject and protocol-currentness boundary;
- LLM execution-resource boundary;
- repository/worktree and publication boundary;
- configuration and secret-injection boundary;
- human-decision interaction boundary;
- global invariants;
- cross-cutting failure policy;
- exact final output contract;
- orchestrator pseudocode;
- dependency inventory.

The System Brief must distinguish required campaign capabilities from chosen module boundaries.

## Exclusions

This Issue does not:

- implement production code;
- create RED tests;
- define internal module algorithms belonging in NIB-M;
- write the `llm-runtime` Dependency Contract;
- select real reviewer profiles;
- execute any real campaign;
- create TURNLOCK semantics.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] One active NIB-S exists at the exact repository path selected by the architect.
- [x] The NIB-S satisfies the workspace Normative Implementation Brief system-brief contract.
- [x] The complete implementation pipeline and execution order are explicit.
- [x] Every module boundary has exact consumed and produced types.
- [x] All global invariants and cross-cutting policies required by the accepted Gate A contract are explicit.
- [x] Persistence, sealing, restart, idempotence, concurrency, worktree, publication, configuration, secret, LLM-runtime, and human-decision boundaries are explicit.
- [x] The top-level output contract is exact.
- [x] The orchestrator is specified in implementable pseudocode.
- [x] The dependency inventory is explicit.
- [x] No internal module algorithm is incorrectly embedded in the System Brief.
- [x] No coding-agent implementation decision remains hidden in the System Brief's scope.
- [x] Every material discovery exposed while writing the NIB-S is classified and routed before acceptance.
- [x] No production implementation is added.

## Validation

Validate NIB metadata and structure against the current workspace NIB convention.

Perform an architect review for decisional completeness, inter-boundary coherence, non-ambiguity, and consistency with ADR-041 through ADR-048.

## Authoritative references

- `docs/formal/gate-a-campaign-runner-construction-plan.md`
- `formal/verification.yaml`
- `formal/reviews/README.md`
- ADR-041 through ADR-048

## Problem

The System Brief can define the runner's module boundaries, but the coding agent must not infer each module's algorithms, signatures, state transitions, errors, or edge-case behavior during GREEN.

Every module selected by the accepted NIB-S therefore requires a decisional-complete Module Brief before production implementation.

## Current authoritative boundary

Module Briefs consume the accepted NIB-S and existing TURNLOCK hostile-review authority.

They may implement accepted behavior but may not introduce product semantics, alter the campaign protocol, or invent behavior to accommodate a provider or implementation convenience.

## Scope

Produce the complete set of active NIB-M documents required by the accepted NIB-S.

For every module selected by NIB-S, the architect must specify:

- exact responsibility;
- exact public/internal signatures required by the construction design;
- exact input/output types;
- state read/write ownership;
- implementable algorithm/pseudocode;
- deterministic versus cognitive operations;
- error taxonomy and propagation;
- retry behavior where applicable;
- idempotence behavior;
- edge cases;
- invariants;
- interactions with sealed artifacts and campaign state;
- required dependency-contract references;
- behavior under interruption/restart where relevant;
- forbidden behavior.

If one accepted module has multiple independently coherent responsibilities that require separate briefs, the architect determines and records that decomposition. The coding agent does not.

## Exclusions

This Issue does not:

- write production code;
- write the NIB-T;
- implement tests;
- choose new module boundaries contrary to the accepted NIB-S;
- create dependency behavior not already selected by NIB-S;
- select real reviewer profiles;
- execute a real campaign.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Every implementation module selected by NIB-S has sufficient active NIB-M coverage.
- [ ] Module signatures and cross-module types are coherent with NIB-S.
- [ ] Every branch that production code must implement is determined by explicit module behavior or an authoritative dependency contract.
- [ ] Failure, retry, interruption, restart, and edge-case behavior are explicit where applicable.
- [ ] State ownership and mutation boundaries are explicit.
- [ ] No module brief requires the coding agent to choose campaign semantics.
- [ ] External non-trivial dependency usage is explicitly identified for Dependency Contract coverage.
- [ ] Inter-NIB coherence is checked across the complete module set.
- [ ] Every material discovery is classified and routed before acceptance.
- [ ] No production implementation is added.

## Validation

Perform an architect review for decisional completeness, non-ambiguity, exact compatibility with the accepted NIB-S, and complete coverage of the selected module architecture.

## Authoritative references

- accepted Gate A campaign runner NIB-S
- `docs/formal/gate-a-campaign-runner-construction-plan.md`
- `formal/verification.yaml`
- `formal/reviews/README.md`
- ADR-041 through ADR-049

## Problem

The campaign runner must enter construction through observable failing tests rather than by implementing production code and retrofitting tests afterwards.

The RED contract must be complete enough to detect semantic shortcuts, evidence-integrity bypasses, retry cherry-picking, recovery defects, and improper human escalation without coupling the suite to internal function structure.

## Current authoritative boundary

NIB-T verifies observable implementation behavior derived from the accepted NIB-S, NIB-M set, Dependency Contracts, and hostile-review authority.

It does not create new runner behavior.

## Scope

Produce the active NIB-T for the complete initial campaign runner.

It must define executable acceptance vectors, property/anti-cheat tests, contract invariants, fixtures, and helpers covering at least:

- clean campaign progression;
- exact subject/protocol input binding;
- protocol-currentness failure;
- stale semantic subject;
- provider technical failure before completion;
- structurally invalid reviewer completion followed by valid retry where allowed;
- rejection of semantic-result retry/cherry-picking;
- effective reviewer-identity alias collision;
- sealed raw artifact immutability;
- duplicate/collision protection;
- crash before and after sealing boundaries;
- crash before and after durable journal transitions;
- restart without duplicate reviewer execution;
- one-to-one finding normalization;
- materiality challenge success and objection survival;
- refutation challenge success and objection survival;
- failure-to-refute not becoming proof of finding truth;
- derived correction challenge;
- normative-impact challenge;
- decision-necessity challenge;
- genuine `DECISION-REQUIRED`;
- technical/assurance uncertainty becoming `OPERATOR-ACTION-REQUIRED`;
- exact repair authorization and challenge;
- rejection of patch content outside authorization;
- semantic-subject recomputation after repair;
- protocol change/staleness behavior;
- stale-protocol finding re-adjudication;
- dirty parent workspace isolation;
- concurrent repository change/precondition failure;
- publication failure;
- interruption/recovery;
- secret non-disclosure in durable artifacts;
- exact top-level external outcomes.

Tests must target observable behavior and public construction contracts, not implementation-private helper functions.

## Exclusions

NIB-T does not:

- prescribe internal module data structures;
- prescribe private helper functions;
- add production implementation;
- contain surface-only tests that are green before behavior exists;
- execute a real Gate A campaign.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] One active NIB-T covers the complete initial runner behavior selected by NIB-S/NIB-M.
- [ ] Every module edge case with observable consequences has corresponding behavioral coverage.
- [ ] Anti-cheat properties cover the known assurance bypass classes.
- [ ] Tests do not depend on real provider credentials unless explicitly isolated as later live conformance work.
- [ ] RED can be established with compilation scaffolding only.
- [ ] No prescribed NIB-T behavioral test should pass merely because a symbol, constant, fixture, or mock helper exists.
- [ ] Inter-NIB type and behavior coherence is verified.
- [ ] Every material discovery is classified and routed before acceptance.

## Validation

Perform architect review of all vectors against the accepted System Brief, Module Briefs, Dependency Contracts, and current hostile-review protocol.

## Authoritative references

- accepted NIB-S
- accepted NIB-M set
- accepted Dependency Contracts
- `docs/formal/gate-a-campaign-runner-construction-plan.md`
- `formal/reviews/README.md`
- ADR-041 through ADR-048

## Problem

A construction pack can be internally detailed while still containing contradictions, omitted behavior, hidden decisions, unimplementable boundaries, weak test coverage, or places where the coding agent would have to improvise.

The complete construction pack must therefore be attacked before RED.

## Current authoritative boundary

Workspace hostile specification review is a construction-quality mechanism only.

It is not Gate A hostile-review evidence, does not count as a qualifying Gate A reviewer execution, and does not alter protocol identity `P`.

The coding agent may mechanically execute the hostile-review tooling and return its raw findings. The architect owns interpretation, discovery classification, correction design, and final disposition.

## Scope

Hostile-review the complete active campaign-runner construction pack:

- NIB-S;
- every NIB-M;
- every required Dependency Contract;
- NIB-T.

Attack at least:

- uncovered implementation decisions;
- inconsistent types/interfaces across briefs;
- ambiguous branch behavior;
- missing failure or restart behavior;
- hidden semantic assumptions;
- implementation convenience masquerading as protocol meaning;
- missing anti-cheat tests;
- invalid RED expectations;
- dependency-contract gaps;
- secret-handling ambiguity;
